Since ADR 0008, skeid.yaml names customers by derived key id (k_<12 hex>, from `skeid keyid`). It works and it keeps customer keys out of config, but it is unreadable and makes key rotation a config edit.

The natural home for a key -> name mapping is next to the key in OpenBao (secret/skeid/customer/<name> already exists in the example stack). The KeyBroker could resolve a presented key to its customer name, and policies could be named by that.

Blocked on karr #3: the KeyBroker blocks the event loop and has no cache. A per-request vault round-trip on the routing path is not acceptable -- ADR 0008 says a request costs one hash lookup, and this would be the thing that breaks it. Do #3 first.

Open question worth deciding before building: does policy assignment then belong in OpenBao next to the key, or stay in YAML keyed by name? ADR 0008 leaves this open on purpose.

2026-09-10 02:02 Implemented (commit 800333f). Decision: YAML by name, NOT OpenBao (per user). Shape: optional top-level names: section mapping readable name -> customer key id; keys: entries may be written by name or raw id; resolved to ids once at config load (key_names + resolve_key_id in _load_policies), erased before the request path -- policy_for_key still one hash lookup, no vault call. Load-time croaks: name->structure, name->empty id, two keys: labels resolving to one id. key_id_for_name accessor added. Tests in t/26 (named entry -> mapped id, raw-id alongside, name is not an identity, config errors croak); full suite 636 green. ADR 0011 records it and forward-points 0008; awaiting your review. No design ambiguity needed flagging -- the name->id map shape is the direct reading of the user decision (alternatives considered: keys: keyed by name with inline id; separate registry was cleaner and least invasive to the request path).

